<p class="lede">You will build a word counter: it reads a text file, counts each word, and prints the most common first, in five steps; you run it after each one.</p>

<p>This chapter uses forms later chapters explain: <code>struct</code>, <code>Map</code>, <code>List</code>, functions and errors. Type and run the programs; a new form gets a sentence on what it does, with its chapter linked. Read <a href="book-01-start.html">chapter 1</a> first if you have not run a Bayan program.</p>

<h2 id="plan">The plan</h2>
<p>The program has four jobs:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Read a file into a string.</li>
  <li>Split the string into words, ignoring case and punctuation.</li>
  <li>Count each word.</li>
  <li>Sort the counts, highest first, and print them.</li>
</ol>
<p>Each program first writes <code>sample.txt</code> in its working folder for something to read; the last step reads any file named on the command line instead.</p>

<h2 id="read">Step 1: read a file</h2>
<example name="book-02-read"/>
<p>Run it with <code>bayan run book-02-read.byn</code>, or your file's name. New:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>void main() fails IoError</code> declares the entry point and says it can fail with an <code>IoError</code>; reading and writing files can fail, and Bayan makes you say so in the signature.</li>
  <li><code>try</code> before a call means "if this fails, stop here and pass the failure up"; because <code>main</code> declares <code>fails IoError</code>, passing it up is allowed. <a href="book-08-errors.html">Chapter 8</a> covers errors.</li>
  <li><code>Io.writeFile(path, bytes)</code> writes bytes to a file; <code>"…".bytes</code> is the text as bytes. <code>Io.readText(path)</code> reads a file back as a string.</li>
  <li><code>text.length</code> is the size in bytes; <code>text.lines</code> walks the lines.</li>
  <li><code>var lines = 0</code> is a variable you can change; <code>let</code> would not allow <code>lines += 1</code>. <a href="book-03-basics.html">Chapter 3</a> covers both.</li>
</ul>
<p>Without <code>try</code>, the compiler says the call can fail:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2602:</span> `readText` can fail; handle the error or pass it on
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> nottry.byn:2:16

<span class="gut">  2 │</span>     let text = Io.readText("sample.txt")
<span class="gut">    │</span>                ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: propagate the error with `try`, or handle it with `catch`</span>
<span class="gut">    │ 2 │</span>     let text = try Io.readText("sample.txt")
<span class="gut">    │   │</span><span class="add">                ++++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2602</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check nottry.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>nottry.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Keep <code>try</code> but drop <code>fails IoError</code> from <code>main</code>, and it says the error has nowhere to go:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2604:</span> `main` cannot fail, so it cannot pass on `IoError`
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> nofails.byn:2:16

<span class="gut">  2 │</span>     let text = try Io.readText("sample.txt")
<span class="gut">    │</span>                ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: handle the error with `catch`, or declare `fails IoError`</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2604</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check nofails.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>nofails.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Both messages offer two ways out: pass the error up, or <code>catch</code> it where it happens.</p>

<h2 id="split">Step 2: split the lines into words</h2>
<p>Cut each line at spaces, strip punctuation and ignore case.</p>
<example name="book-02-split"/>
<ul>
  <li><code>line.parts(by: " ")</code> splits a string at every space.</li>
  <li><code>part.trimmed(by: ['.', ',', '!', '?'])</code> strips any of those characters from both ends. A character literal uses single quotes; <code>[…]</code> with a type from the parameter is a list of them.</li>
  <li><code>.lowercased()</code> returns a lowercase copy, so <code>The</code> and <code>the</code> are one word.</li>
  <li><code>continue if word.isEmpty</code> is a postfix <code>if</code> that skips the rest of the pass for an empty word, same as <code>if word.isEmpty { continue }</code> on one line.</li>
</ul>
<p>Notice what you did not write: no word-boundary helper, no character loop. Bayan strings are UTF-8, and the string methods handle it.</p>

<h2 id="count">Step 3: count the words</h2>
<p><code>Map&lt;string, i32&gt;</code> maps a string to a 32-bit integer: word as key, count as value.</p>
<example name="book-02-count"/>
<ul>
  <li><code>Map&lt;string, i32&gt;()</code> makes an empty map; the type goes in the angle brackets.</li>
  <li><code>counts.tryGet(at: word)</code> looks a key up and gives <code>i32?</code>: a number, or <code>null</code> for "not there". <code>?? 0</code> turns <code>null</code> into <code>0</code>, so the line reads: the new count is the old count, or zero, plus one.</li>
  <li><code>for e in counts</code> goes through the entries, each with a <code>.key</code> and <code>.value</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The output follows first appearance, not count: a Bayan <code>Map</code> remembers insertion order.</p>
<p>Why not <code>counts[word] += 1</code>? Reading an absent key is a bug, so it stops the program: a panic, not an error you can handle:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre>panic: key not found in map
  at mapmissing2.byn:4</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan run mapmissing2.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>mapmissing2.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The panic names the line. <code>tryGet</code> is the checked version, returning <code>null</code>.</p>

<h2 id="sort">Step 4: sort the counts</h2>
<p><code>Map</code> has no order by count. You need a list of (word, count) pairs and a way to order them. Define a <code>struct</code> for a pair:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Entry {
    string word
    i32 count

    bool isBefore(Entry other) {
        if count != other.count { return count &gt; other.count }
        return word &lt; other.word
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>A struct groups named fields. <code>Entry</code>'s <code>isBefore</code> method says whether this entry comes before another: higher count first, then the alphabetically first word. Strings compare byte by byte, so capitals sort before lowercase; our words are all lowercase, so it does not matter. <a href="book-05-structs.html">Chapter 5</a> covers structs.</p>
<p>The standard <code>sort()</code> works on lists of numbers and strings; sorting your own type by a rule is not available yet, so you write the sort. An insertion sort is short: take each entry and swap it left while it belongs before its neighbour:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void sortEntries(ref List&lt;Entry&gt; entries) {
    for i in 1..entries.length {
        var j = i
        while j &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; entries[j].isBefore(entries[j - 1]) {
            let tmp = entries[j]
            entries[j] = entries[j - 1]
            entries[j - 1] = tmp
            j -= 1
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
<p><code>ref List&lt;Entry&gt;</code> means the function changes the caller's list in place; the call says <code>ref</code> too: <code>sortEntries(ref entries)</code>. <a href="book-04-functions.html">Chapter 4</a> covers this. <code>1..entries.length</code> is a range from 1 to before the length.</p>
<p>The loop is quadratic: fine for a few thousand distinct words; a large corpus wants a better sort once <code>sort(by:)</code> arrives.</p>

<h2 id="together">Step 5: put it together</h2>
<p>Now the whole program, with three changes: counting moved into <code>countWords</code>; <code>main</code> takes the command-line arguments, using the first as the path when given; and prints a summary line first.</p>
<example name="book-02-words"/>
<p>New parts:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>void main(ReadOnlySpan&lt;string&gt; args)</code> receives the arguments after the program name; <code>ReadOnlySpan&lt;string&gt;</code> is a read-only view of strings, with a <code>length</code>, indexed as <code>args[0]</code>.</li>
  <li><code>countWords</code> has no <code>uses</code> clause, being private to the file. A <code>public</code> function must declare when it allocates memory, so a public <code>countWords</code> would need <code>uses alloc</code>; even one making an empty map is refused:</li>
</ul>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6002:</span> `emptyCounts` uses `alloc` but does not declare it
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> publicalloc.byn:2:12

<span class="gut">  2 │</span>     return Map&lt;string, i32&gt;()
<span class="gut">    │</span>            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ uses `alloc`
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: add `alloc` to the `uses` clause, or remove the call</span>
<span class="gut">    │ 1 │</span> public Map&lt;string, i32&gt; emptyCounts() uses alloc {
<span class="gut">    │   │</span><span class="add">                                      +++++++++++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN6002</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check publicalloc.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>publicalloc.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><a href="book-14-effects.html">Chapter 14</a> explains effects.</p>
<p>Run it on your file:</p>
<pre><code>bayan run book-02-words.byn -- notes.txt</code></pre>
<p>If the file is missing, <code>main</code>'s <code>try</code> passes an <code>IoError</code> out of the program, which prints a message and exits with status 1:</p>
<pre><code>error: The file does not exist.</code></pre>

<h3 id="as-a-project">As a project</h3>
<p>To keep it, make a project (<a href="book-01-start.html#projects">chapter 1</a>) and paste the program into <code>src/app/main.byn</code>. It reads a file, so add the file-system capability to <code>package.byn</code> as chapter 1 showed:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">capabilities: [.fs],</code></pre>
<p>Then <code>bayan run</code> runs it; <code>bayan run -- notes.txt</code> passes the name through.</p>

<h2 id="try-next">Make it yours</h2>
<p>Each needs only what you have seen:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Print only the top 3 words: loop over <code>0..3</code>, indexing <code>entries[i]</code>. What if there are fewer than three? Fix it with <code>min</code>-style logic of your own.</li>
  <li>Strip more punctuation: <code>;</code>, <code>:</code>, quotes.</li>
  <li>Count lines too, using step 1's loop.</li>
  <li>Count characters instead of words: <code>for ch in text</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<example name="book-02-q1" output="no"/>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>What does this program print?</summary>
  <p>Prints:</p>
<pre><code>b 3
a 2
c 1</code></pre>
  <p>The map keeps first-appearance order (<code>b</code>, <code>a</code>, <code>c</code>), not sorting by key or count. A common wrong answer, <code>a 2</code> first, suits a sorted map; Bayan's <code>Map</code> is not one.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>In <code>counts[word] = (counts.tryGet(at: word) ?? 0) + 1</code>, what does <code>?? 0</code> do, and what happens to an unseen word without it?</summary>
  <p><code>tryGet</code> returns <code>i32?</code>; <code>??</code> picks the right side when the left is <code>null</code>, so a new word starts from 0. Without <code>?? 0</code> the value might be missing, and <code>1</code> cannot be added to that. <code>counts[word]</code> compiles but panics on the first new word: absent keys panic. <code>tryGet</code> asks "is it there?" without stopping the program.</p>
</details>
<example name="book-02-q2" output="no"/>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>What does this line print?</summary>
  <p>Prints:</p>
<pre><code>true true true false</code></pre>
  <p>Strings compare by bytes, not dictionary order: <code>"fig" &lt; "pear"</code> because <code>f</code> precedes <code>p</code>; <code>"Yusuf" &lt; "apple"</code> because a capital has a lower byte than lowercase; <code>"a" &lt; "ab"</code> because a prefix is smaller; <code>"b" &lt; "ab"</code> is false because <code>b</code> is greater than <code>a</code>. So step 2 lowercases every word before counting.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>You delete <code>fails IoError</code> from <code>main</code> but leave the <code>try</code> calls. What does the compiler do?</summary>
  <p>It refuses to build (<code>BYN2604</code>): <code>main</code> cannot fail, so it cannot pass on an <code>IoError</code>. The help line offers two fixes: restore <code>fails IoError</code>, or <code>catch</code> the error in place. It neither ignores the error silently nor turns it into a crash.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Why does <code>sortEntries</code> take <code>ref List&lt;Entry&gt;</code> and not <code>List&lt;Entry&gt;</code>?</summary>
  <p>A parameter without <code>ref</code> is read-only: the function cannot change it. <code>sortEntries</code> swaps elements, so it needs <code>ref</code> in the signature and at the call; without it the compiler rejects the swaps. The call marker tells <code>main</code>'s reader that <code>entries</code> may change.</p>
</details>

